@meta
id: 31-07
title: Drill — write the keymap, with a desc
chapter: 31
type: drill
skills: lua-config
par: 92
minutes: 6
reps: 8
vscode: nothing — JSON has no description field you would ever fill in
open: init.lua

@brief
Eight times: port this mapping to the end of the file and save.

    nnoremap <leader>q :bd<CR>          desc: Close the buffer

The description is part of the drill. Write it every rep, even when it
feels like noise, until leaving it out feels wrong.

Graded in vim. The Lua is written, not run.

@keys
| `G` `o` | open a line at the bottom |
| `vim.keymap.set('n', lhs, rhs, { desc = '...' })` | the shape |

@hints
Mode, left side, right side, options table. Four arguments, in that order.
--
`:bd<CR>` goes inside quotes exactly as written — vim reads `<CR>`, you do
not press Enter.

@solution
    G o vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>q', ':bd<CR>', { desc = 'Close the buffer' }) <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Seventy-eight keystrokes, most of it punctuation, which is precisely why
it is worth drilling. The commas, the quote pairs and the braces are where
every hand-written Lua config breaks, and a syntax error in `init.lua`
means Neovim starts with none of your settings and a stack trace instead.

If you never want to type it again: `vim.keymap.set` is what snippets and
`:h lua-guide-mappings` are for, and most people end up with a local
helper of their own — `local map = vim.keymap.set` at the top of the file
turns every line below it into `map('n', ...)`. Earn the shortcut by
drilling the long form first.

@start init.lua
-- Mappings
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>h', ':nohlsearch<CR>', { desc = 'Clear search highlight' })

@expected init.lua
-- Mappings
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>h', ':nohlsearch<CR>', { desc = 'Clear search highlight' })
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>q', ':bd<CR>', { desc = 'Close the buffer' })
